> Create smooth curves from control points, with Bézier, B-spline, Catmull-Rom, Hermite and linear interpolation.

name Spline
slug bookshelf-spline
version 5.0.0
tags runtime
authors Aksiome

$points: [[number] @ 1..3] @ 2..

$axis[x]:
  nth: first
$axis[y]:
  nth: second
$axis[z]:
  nth: third

$spline[bezier]:
  name: Bézier spline
  since: 2025/01/25 1.21.4
  points: control points, 4 for the first segment and 3 more for each next one: the last point of a segment starts the next
  segment: control points of the segment, 4 of them, any further point is ignored

$spline[bspline]:
  name: B-spline
  since: 2025/01/25 1.21.4
  points: control points, 4 for the first segment and 1 more for each next one: a segment shares 3 points with the next, which keeps the curve smooth
  segment: control points of the segment, 4 of them, any further point is ignored

$spline[catmull_rom]:
  name: Catmull-Rom spline
  since: 2025/01/25 1.21.4
  points: control points, 4 for the first segment and 1 more for each next one: a segment shares 3 points with the next, and the curve goes through every point but the first and the last
  segment: control points of the segment, 4 of them: the segment goes from the second to the third, any further point is ignored

$spline[hermite]:
  name: Hermite spline
  since: 2025/01/25 1.21.4
  points: positions and tangents, in absolute coordinates: `[p0, t0, p1, t1]` for the first segment and a position and its tangent for each next one, the last position and tangent of a segment starting the next
  segment: start, start tangent, end and end tangent of the segment, the tangents as absolute points, any further point is ignored

$spline[linear]:
  name: linear spline
  since: 2025/05/14 1.21.5
  points: points, 2 for the first segment and 1 more for each next one: each pair of consecutive points is joined by a straight line
  segment: start and end of the segment, any further point is ignored


feature function evaluate/$spline > Get the point of a $spline.name at a given time.
  since $spline.since
  input arguments
    points: $points > $spline.points
    time: number @ 0.. > position on the spline, from 0 to the number of segments: the integer part picks the segment, the fraction the point within it
  output storage: [float] @ 1..3 > point at the given time, with as many coordinates as the points of the spline

feature function sample/$spline > Get points spread along a $spline.name, at regular steps.
  since $spline.since
  input arguments
    points: $points > $spline.points
    step: number @ 0<..1 > fraction of a segment between two points: smaller values give more points
  output storage: [[float] @ 1..3] > points from the start to the end of the spline, `step` apart

feature context_float_provider $spline/$axis > Get the $axis coordinate of a point on one segment of a $spline.name. The segment is overwritten by `evaluate/$spline` and `sample/$spline`.
  since 2026/11/10 26.4
  input storage
    points: $points > $spline.segment
    time: number @ 0..1 > position on the segment, from 0 at its start to 1 at its end
  output result: float > $axis coordinate of the point, the $axis.nth coordinate of the control points
